## Why This Is Asked
Assess system design, data governance, and production-readiness for cross-tenant AI in security contexts.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant RBAC, per-tenant quotas
- KB-grounded prompts via Cognitive Search
- PII/PHI redaction with Text Analytics
- Canary rollouts behind API Management
- Data residency, auditing, and cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-routing: validate tenant, fetch KB version, route to OpenAI with context
async function handleRequest(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const policy = await getTenantPolicy(tenant);
  const kb = await fetchKBVersion(tenant);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(req.body, kb, policy);
  return callOpenAI(prompt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-tenant audit logging and tamper-evidence in Data Lake?
- What tests would you run to verify canary prompt rollouts do not regress SLAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical APIM quota implementation and OpenAI routing for multi-tenant SaaS architectures.

## Key Concepts
- API Management policies with rate-limit-by-key
- Tenant-scoped routing via request headers
- Telemetry integration with Application Insights

## Code Example
```xml
<policies>
  <inbound>
    <base />
    <set-variable name='TenantID' value='@(context.Request.Headers.GetValueOrDefault("x-tenant-id","default"))' />
    <rate-limit-by-key calls='60' renewal-period='60' key='@(context.Variables["TenantID"])' />
  </inbound>
</policies>
```

```javascript
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = req.headers['x-tenant-id'] || 'default';
  const startTime = Date.now();

  try {
    const response = await callOpenAI(req.body);

    // Log usage to Application Insights
    context.log('TenantUsage', {
      tenantId,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      duration: Date.now() - startTime,
      status: 'success'
    });

    return { status: 200, body: response };
  } catch (error) {
    context.log.error('TenantError', { tenantId, error: error.message });
    return { status: 500, body: { error: 'Service unavailable' } };
  }
};
```

## Validation Approach
Test with multiple tenant IDs using load testing tools, verify rate limits trigger at 60 requests/minute per tenant, and confirm telemetry appears in Application Insights queries.

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design governance-aware AI copilots that enforce enterprise privacy policies, implement policy-as-code, and ensure auditable, defensible decisions in multi-tenant environments.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI integration with API Management for secure API exposure
- Cognitive Search knowledge base grounding against versioned Azure Blob storage
- Policy-as-code modules for privacy, consent, and data retention enforcement
- Per-tenant Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) via Azure Active Directory
- Comprehensive auditable logging in Azure Data Lake or equivalent storage
- End-to-end test harness for validation across all components

## Code Example
```javascript
function evaluatePolicy(request, kb, policy) {
  const redactedRequest = redactPII(request, policy.piiRules);
  const groundedContext = await searchKnowledgeBase(redactedRequest, kb);
  const consentCheck = validateConsent(redactedRequest, policy.consentRules);
  const retentionCheck = validateRetention(redactedRequest, policy.retentionRules);

  if (!consentCheck.valid || !retentionCheck.valid) {
    return { decision: 'DENY', rationale: [...consentCheck.reasons, ...retentionCheck.reasons] };
  }

  const decision = await openai.chat.completions.create({
    messages: [{ role: 'system', content: policy.determinationPrompt },
               { role: 'user', content: JSON.stringify({ request: redactedRequest, context: groundedContext }) }]
  });

  return parseDecision(decision);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
